      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul class="sources">
        <li>NIST/SEMATECH, <a href="https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda352.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">One-sample t-test</a> and <a href="https://www.itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/eda/section3/eda353.htm" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Two-sample t-test</a> (formulas and degrees of freedom).</li>
        <li>OpenStax, <a href="https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics/pages/8-2-a-single-population-mean-using-the-student-t-distribution" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The Student t-Distribution</a> (heavier tails, t vs z, the Gosset story).</li>
        <li>Wasserstein and Lazar, <a href="https://sciences.ucf.edu/biology/d4lab/wp-content/uploads/sites/23/2017/03/WassersteinLazar2016.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">The ASA Statement on p-Values</a>, The American Statistician, 2016.</li>
