        <li>Cohen's standards, <a href="https://stats.libretexts.org/Bookshelves/Applied_Statistics/Business_Statistics_(OpenStax)/10%3A_Hypothesis_Testing_with_Two_Samples/10.02%3A_Cohen's_Standards_for_Small_Medium_and_Large_Effect_Sizes" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Effect-size thresholds</a> (0.2 / 0.5 / 0.8).</li>
        <li>Delacre, Lakens and Leys, <a href="https://rips-irsp.com/articles/10.5334/irsp.82" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Why to use Welch's t-test by default</a>, International Review of Social Psychology, 2017.</li>
        <li>Duke Global Health Institute, <a href="https://sites.globalhealth.duke.edu/rdac/wp-content/uploads/sites/27/2020/08/Core-Guide_Multiple-Comparisons-Part-I_01-11-18.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Multiple Testing, Part 1</a> (about 40% false-positive rate over 10 tests).</li>
